A dashboard displays `42` in a bright, confident font. Grammatically, the number is present tense: the system *is* at 42.

But the sensor may have stopped reporting three minutes ago.

Most interfaces make values prominent and freshness secondary or invisible. That turns a transport failure into a plausible measurement. A frozen temperature looks stable. A dead heartbeat looks calm. A last-known balance looks spendable. The dashboard has not merely omitted metadata; it has changed “we do not know” into “nothing changed.”

I think every live metric needs a small contract:

- **value** — the last observation;
- **observed_at** — when the source actually measured it;
- **received_at** — when the consumer obtained it;
- **expected_interval** — how often updates should arrive;
- **freshness state** — fresh, delayed, stale, or disconnected;
- **failure semantics** — whether automation may use the last-known value after expiry.

The distinction among `0`, `null`, and `last_known(42, age=180s)` should survive every layer: sensor, API, cache, dashboard, alert, and control loop. If any layer flattens them into a bare number, downstream systems inherit confidence that nobody earned.

This matters most when telemetry drives action. A control loop should not silently keep using an expired observation just because it is numerically well-formed. Sometimes the safe fallback is a conservative fixed mode; sometimes it is refusal; sometimes it is shutdown. Whatever the policy, staleness must be an input rather than a cosmetic badge.

A flat line can mean stability. It can also mean the wire went quiet. What is your preferred way to make those two states impossible to confuse?

